LAB — DOCKER COMPOSE (Step-by-step)
Preparation (one-time)

Open terminal

Windows: Open PowerShell or Git Bash.

Mac/Linux: Open Terminal.

Verify Docker & Compose installed

docker --version
docker compose version


If either command fails, install Docker Desktop (Windows/Mac) or Docker Engine + Compose Plugin (Linux).

Workspace setup

Create lab folder

# Mac/Linux
mkdir -p ~/compose-lab && cd ~/compose-lab

# Windows PowerShell
mkdir C:\compose-lab
cd C:\compose-lab
# or in Git Bash:
cd /c/compose-lab


Open your editor (VS Code recommended)

# from terminal if code CLI available:
code .


Or open the folder in your preferred editor.

TASK 1 — Basic compose (nginx + postgres)
1. Create docker-compose.yml

Create a file named docker-compose.yml in compose-lab with this exact content:

version: "3.9"

services:
  web:
    image: nginx:latest
    ports:
      - "8080:80"

  db:
    image: postgres:15
    environment:
      POSTGRES_USER: demo
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: demo
      POSTGRES_DB: demo_db


(Save the file.)

2. Start services (in terminal, inside compose-lab)
docker compose up -d

3. Verify
docker compose ps              # shows running services
docker ps                      # shows running containers


Open browser → http://localhost:8080 → Nginx welcome page should appear.
Take a screenshot of the browser + docker compose ps output.

TASK 2 — Add Redis + depends_on
1. Edit docker-compose.yml to add Redis and depends_on

Replace web service block with:

services:
  web:
    image: nginx:latest
    ports:
      - "8080:80"
    depends_on:
      - redis

  db:
    image: postgres:15
    environment:
      POSTGRES_USER: demo
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: demo
      POSTGRES_DB: demo_db

  redis:
    image: redis:alpine


Save the file.

2. Restart compose (apply changes)
docker compose up -d

3. Verify
docker compose ps
# Expect: web, db, redis all listed as up
docker logs <container_name>   # optional, e.g. docker logs compose-lab-redis-1


Take screenshots of docker compose ps showing three services.

TASK 3 — Deploy on another machine (brief steps for handoff)

Zip the folder

# Mac/Linux
cd ~/ && zip -r compose-lab.zip compose-lab

# Windows PowerShell (from parent folder)
Compress-Archive -Path .\compose-lab\* -DestinationPath .\compose-lab.zip


Transfer compose-lab.zip to target machine (USB / SCP / Google Drive).

On target machine open terminal, unzip and run:

unzip compose-lab.zip   # or extract in File Explorer
cd compose-lab
docker compose up -d
docker compose ps


Verify http://<target-machine-ip>:8080 shows Nginx (if ports and firewall permit).

TASK 4 — Add custom network & persistent volume
1. Update docker-compose.yml (replace whole file with this)
version: "3.9"

services:
  web:
    image: nginx:latest
    ports:
      - "8080:80"
    depends_on:
      - db
    networks:
      - app-net

  db:
    image: postgres:15
    environment:
      POSTGRES_USER: demo
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: demo
      POSTGRES_DB: demo_db
    volumes:
      - db-data:/var/lib/postgresql/data
    networks:
      - app-net

  redis:
    image: redis:alpine
    networks:
      - app-net

networks:
  app-net:

volumes:
  db-data:


Save.

2. Bring up services
docker compose up -d

3. Verify network & volume
docker network ls
docker network inspect compose-lab_app-net   # shows connected containers
docker volume ls
docker volume inspect compose-lab_db-data

4. Check data persistence

Enter DB (Postgres) container and create some test data (optional):

# find db container name
docker compose ps
# exec into postgres (psql client not present in image by default); you can run a temporary psql client:
docker run --rm -it --network compose-lab_app-net postgres:15 psql -h db -U demo -d demo_db
# When prompted, enter password: demo
# In psql:
CREATE TABLE testdata (id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY, name TEXT);
INSERT INTO testdata (name) VALUES ('labtest');
SELECT * FROM testdata;
\q


Now stop & remove containers (keeping volumes):

docker compose down


Start again:

docker compose up -d


Re-run the psql client and SELECT * FROM testdata; to confirm data persists.

Take screenshots demonstrating data before down, after up.

TASK 5 — Faster iteration: Flask app with build
1. Replace web with a build service (create files)

In compose-lab create app.py with:

from flask import Flask
app = Flask(__name__)

@app.route("/")
def home():
    return "Hello from Flask + Docker!"

if __name__ == "__main__":
    app.run(host="0.0.0.0", port=5000)


Create Dockerfile (same folder) with:

FROM python:3.10-slim
WORKDIR /app
COPY app.py /app/
RUN pip install --no-cache-dir flask
CMD ["python", "app.py"]


Update docker-compose.yml to:

version: "3.9"

services:
  web:
    build: .
    ports:
      - "5000:5000"
    depends_on:
      - db
    networks:
      - app-net

  db:
    image: postgres:15
    environment:
      POSTGRES_USER: demo
      POSTGRES_PASSWORD: demo
      POSTGRES_DB: demo_db
    volumes:
      - db-data:/var/lib/postgresql/data
    networks:
      - app-net

networks:
  app-net:

volumes:
  db-data:

2. Build & start
docker compose up --build -d

3. Verify

Open browser → http://localhost:5000 → should show Hello from Flask + Docker!
Take screenshot.

4. Edit & update quickly

Edit app.py to:

    return "Hello Docker Compose!"


Rebuild/start (no need to down):

docker compose up --build -d


Refresh http://localhost:5000 → new message should appear.

Useful Commands (copy-paste checklist)
# See running containers
docker compose ps
docker ps

# Logs
docker compose logs web
docker compose logs -f         # follow logs

# Enter container shell (if image has shell)
docker exec -it <container_name> sh
docker exec -it <container_name> bash   # if bash exists

# Stop/Start/Down
docker compose stop
docker compose start
docker compose down

# Remove volumes (careful, deletes data)
docker compose down -v

# Scale a service
docker compose up -d --scale web=2

# Show networks and inspect
docker network ls
docker network inspect compose-lab_app-net

# Show volumes
docker volume ls
docker volume inspect compose-lab_db-data

# Rebuild a single service
docker compose build web
docker compose up -d web

# Remove images you built
docker images
docker rmi <image_id_or_name>

# Save/zip project for transfer
zip -r compose-lab.zip compose-lab
# or on Windows:
Compress-Archive -Path .\compose-lab\* -DestinationPath .\compose-lab.zip

Cleanup at end of lab
# Stop and remove containers, networks (keep volumes)
docker compose down

# If you want to remove volumes too (destroys DB data)
docker compose down -v

# Remove images you built (example)
docker rmi compose-lab_web:latest   # or the image name from docker images

d only for startup ordering; use retry logic in app or wait-for scripts for robust startups.
